<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Coahuiltecan peoples</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Coahuiltecans</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Coahuiltecans is the name given to many small groups of hunter-gatherers who lived in southern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/texas">Texas</a> and north-eastern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mexico">Mexico</a>, in present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/coahuila">Coahuila</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nuevo-leon">Nuevo León</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tamaulipas">Tamaulipas</a>, before and during Spanish colonisation. They lived by gathering plants such as prickly pear and mesquite and by hunting. Epidemics, slave raids and missions, including those at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-antonio">San Antonio</a>, led to their disappearance as distinct peoples by the early 19th century.</p></div>
